{
  "teamId": 110,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Baltimore Orioles were a team in transition, caught between the wreckage of a rebuilding roster and the first faint flickers of a brighter future. They finished 52-110, one of the worst records in baseball, surrendering nearly a thousand runs while their pitching staff posted a ghastly 5.84 ERA. Yet the season was not without its reasons for hope, as young talents like Cedric Mullins and Austin Hays emerged as genuine big-league contributors, and the offense, powered by 195 home runs, occasionally showed what this roster might eventually become.\n\nThe Orioles stumbled out of the gate in April, going 12-14 in a month where opposing batters routinely exploited a thin bullpen. César Valdez and Travis Lakins became recurring villains in the highlight reel of hurt, surrendering clutch hits at the worst possible moments. But even in those early struggles, there were sparks — DJ Stewart's walk-off single in mid-April and Cedric Mullins launching home runs with the confidence of a star in the making offered glimpses of something real amid the losing.\n\nMay and June brought the season's cruelest stretch, a combined 15-40 record that effectively ended any pretense of competitiveness. The bullpen continued to hemorrhage runs, and the rotation provided little shelter. The All-Star break offered no reset — July brought a crushing three-run homer allowed by Tyler Wells against the White Sox that swung nearly half a win in a single swing, and Tanner Scott's meltdown against Tampa Bay in late July stood as one of the season's most devastating moments, a -0.765 WPA play that captured the franchise's fragility in a single brutal at-bat.\n\nThe final months were a long, painful march toward 110 losses, punctuated by individual brightness. Austin Hays homered for his 20th of the season in September, a genuine milestone for a young outfielder finding his footing. But the pitching staff kept crumbling — Tyler Wells alone was the losing party in two of the season's most damaging plays in September, giving up a pair of crushers to Toronto's loaded lineup. César Valdez's final appearance in this timeline, surrendering a bases-clearing triple to J.T. Realmuto with a -0.790 WPA, felt like a fitting, grim coda to a season defined by its suffering. The Orioles looked ahead to 2022 with Mullins, Hays, and Mountcastle as building blocks — but 2021 itself was a 162-game education in how far this franchise still had to go.",
